    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec requires all 400/409/429 rejection responses to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. Investigating the rejection surface:\n\n- **Bean-validation 400s** and every **business-rule 409/429/400** already went through `ExceptionControllerAdvice`, which returns a Spring `ProblemDetail` (all four members, correct content type) \u2014 already compliant.\n- **The one gap:** `OwnerPostcodePolicy.InvalidPostcodeException` (a `@ResponseStatus(BAD_REQUEST)` rejection) had no dedicated handler, so the catch-all `@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)` swallowed it and returned **500**, not a 400 problem+json. I verified this empirically before and after the fix.\n\n**Fix:** a new, self-contained `PostcodeRejectionAdvice` (`src/main/java/.../rest/advice/PostcodeRejectionAdvice.java`) with a single handler that maps `InvalidPostcodeException` to a 400 `ProblemDetail`. It carries `@Order(HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE)` so its specific handler is consulted before the general advice's catch-all \u2014 while every other exception still falls through to the existing advice unchanged.\n\n**Why this shape (structural impact):** the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice` is a large class (~13 handler methods), so its `WMC_other` is high \u2014 adding a method there would pay for the whole class. Instead I added one small new file whose single method has near-zero surrounding complexity, and touched no existing function. The large advice class and all controllers/policies are untouched.\n\nVerified: the postcode path now returns `400` + `application/problem+json` with `type`/`title`/`status`/`detail`, the provided `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` passes, and the full suite is `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
